  greetings:
  - Long trip. Please make it quick.
  - Good evening, officer. Or is it morning here?
  - Everything should be in order.
  - I've been in that queue for three hours.
  - My cousin said this station is friendly.
  - Is the bar still open after this?
  - Don't look at me like that. I'm just tired.
  - Here. All of it.
  - First time on Kestrel. Is it always this cold?
  - They told me to talk to you.
  - I have a connecting shuttle. Please.
  - You look like you need a drink more than I do.
  - Same as last time, officer.
  - Mind the papers, they got wet.
  - I'm only passing through.
  - Work is work. Where do I go?
  crimes:
  - Armed robbery.
  - Smuggling, three counts.
  - Escaped from a labour camp.
  - Desertion.
  - Forgery of transit papers.
  - Sabotage at a fuel depot.
  - Fraud, Meridian Hub.
  - Assault on an officer.
  standingWanted:
  - name: KAEL DROSS
    id: DX-0013-44
    order: 0
    note: Armed robbery, Aska-3
  letters:
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'The heater rattles again, but it still works if you hit it twice. We hit
      it a lot.


      We count the days. Don''t work too hard.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'The little one drew a picture of your station. It has too many windows
      and a cat on the roof.


      We keep it on the wall.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'Prices went up again at the depot. Everyone says it will get better after
      the winter.


      Nobody says which winter.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'Your mother asks if you eat properly. I told her you do. Do you?


      Write
      back when you can.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'The neighbours left for the inner colonies last week. Their flat is dark
      now.


      We are fine. We just miss you.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'There was a power cut all night. We sat together under the blankets and
      told stories about you.


      They were mostly true.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'The school wants new shoes for the winter term. I said we''ll see.


      Don''t
      worry about it. Just come home.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'I found your old jacket in the cupboard. It still smells like you, a little.


      How
      long now?'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'The radio says the routes are getting worse. Be careful who you let through.


      And
      be careful who you don''t.'
  - title: A letter from home
    body: 'We had soup three days in a row. Good soup, though.


      Save what
      you can. We can wait a bit longer.'
